","The GELA LNH-93-1 study demonstrated that **aggressive chemotherapy (ACVBP followed by consolidation) resulted in a significantly higher complete response rate (CRR) compared to standard chemotherapy (CHOP) plus radiotherapy (RT) in patients ≤60 years with low-risk stages I–II intermediate-risk non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL).** The CRR was 95.7% with ACVBP/consolidation versus 76.7% with CHOP/RT. However, the study also showed that the **ACVBP/consolidation arm had a significantly higher rate of grade 3-4 toxicities** (40.6%) compared to the CHOP/RT arm (15.4%). Notably, the **median progression-free duration (PFS) and median overall survival (OS) are similar between the two groups**, indicating that the higher CRR with ACVBP/consolidation did not translate to a significant survival advantage. The study also showed that the **ACVBP/consolidation arm had a higher rate of second-mission (second-line) chemotherapy** (10.6%) compared to the CHOP/RT arm (2.9%). 

In summary, the GELA LNH-93-1 study demonstrated that ACVBP/consolidation resulted in a higher CRR but also higher toxicity compared to CHOP/RT in patients ≤60 years with low-risk stages I–II intermediate-risk NHL. The median PFS and OS were similar between the two groups, suggesting that the higher CRR with ACVBP/consolidation may not translate to a significant survival advantage.
