","To calculate the Gibbs free energy change (ΔG°) for the electrochemical reaction, we first need to determine the overall cell potential (E°cell) using the standard reduction potentials. The reaction is:

Zn(s) + Cu2+(aq) → Zn2+(aq) + Cu(s)

Since the given potentials are for the reduction half-reactions, we need to reverse the Zn2+|Zn half-reaction to make it an oxidation half-reaction:

Zn(s) → Zn2+(aq) + 2e- (E° = -0.76 V)

Now, we can add the two half-reactions to get the overall reaction:

Zn(s) + Cu2+(aq) → Zn2+(aq) + Cu(s)

The overall cell potential (E°cell) is the difference between the reduction potential of the cathode (Cu2+|Cu) and the oxidation potential of the anode (Zn|Zn2+):

E°cell = E°(Cu2+|Cu) - E°(Zn|Zn2+) = 0.34 V - (-0.76 V) = 1.10 V

Now we can use the Nernst equation to calculate the Gibbs free energy change (ΔG°) at 25°C (298 K):

ΔG° = -nFE°cell

where n is the number of moles of electrons transferred (in this case, n = 2), F is the Faraday constant (96,485 C/mol), and E°cell is the overall cell potential.

ΔG° = -(2 mol)(96,485 C/mol)(1.10 V) = -212,467 J/mol

The Gibbs free energy change (ΔG°) for the electrochemical reaction is -212,467 J/mol.
