","In a double-stranded DNA molecule, the bases pair up as follows: adenine (A) pairs with thymine (T), and guanine (G) pairs with cytosine (C). Therefore, the number of each base in the double-stranded DNA molecule is equal to the total number of bases in the given strand. 

Given the strand has 20A, 25G, 30C, and 22T, the double-stranded DNA molecule will have:
- 20A (since A pairs with T)
- 25G (since G pairs with C)
- 30C (since C pairs with G)
- 22T (since T pairs with A)

Thus, the double-stranded DNA molecule contains $\boxed{20 \text{ A}, 25 \text{ G}, 30 \text{ C}, 22 \text{ T}}$.
