"In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The key difference between σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals lies in the electron density distribution:  
- **σ molecular orbital**: The electron density is distributed symmetrically about the internuclear axis (the line between the nuclei of the bonded atoms).  
- **π molecular orbital**: The electron density is concentrated above and below the internuclear axis, with a node (zero electron density) along the internuclear axis.  

Thus, σ orbitals are formed by head-on overlap of atomic orbitals, while π orbitals are formed by lateral overlap.  

$\boxed{\text{σ orbital: electron density symmetric about the internuclear axis, no node along the axis.}}$  
$\boxed{\text{π orbital: electron density above/below the internuclear axis, node along the axis.}}$
