1. Chain isomerism: This type of isomerism occurs when the carbon atoms in the molecule are arranged in different sequences. For C4H10O, chain isomers are:

   a. Butan-1-ol (CH3CH2CH2CH2OH) - The hydroxyl group is attached to the first carbon of the butane chain.
   b. Butan-2-ol (CH3CH2CH(OH)CH3) - The hydroxyl group is attached to the second carbon of the butane chain.

2. Functional group isomerism: This type of isomerism occurs when the molecule has the same molecular formula but different functional groups. For C4H10O, the functional group isomer is:

   a. Butan-2-one (CH3COCH2CH3) - The molecule contains a carbonyl group (C=O) in the second carbon of the butane chain.

In summary, chain isomers differ in the arrangement of carbon atoms, while functional group isomers have the same carbon skeleton but different functional groups.
