- The reduction of metal oxides by carbon involves the displacement of oxygen by carbon, forming carbon monoxide (CO) or carbon dioxide (CO₂).  
- The reactivity of metal oxides decreases as the ionization energy of the metal increases.  
- Aluminum has a very high ionization energy, making Al₂O₃ highly stable and non-easily reduced by carbon.  
- The other oxides (PbO, ZnO, Fe₂O₃) have metals with lower ionization energies, making them more easily reduced by carbon.  

Thus, Al₂O₃ cannot be reduced to aluminum by carbon under normal conditions.
