1. Concentration of the photosensitizer: The rate of a photochemical reaction is directly proportional to the concentration of the photosensitizer. A higher concentration of the photosensitizer means that more molecules are available to absorb photons and undergo the photochemical reaction, leading to a faster reaction rate.

2. Duration of exposure: The duration of exposure to light also affects the rate of photochemical reactions. As the exposure time increases, more photons are absorbed by the photosensitizer, leading to a higher probability of the photosensitizer undergoing the photochemical reaction. This results in a faster reaction rate.

