"Passing \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas into a mixture of \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions in an acidified aqueous solution precipitates which sulfides?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
Passing \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas into a mixture of \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions in an acidified aqueous solution precipitates which sulfides?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To determine which sulfides precipitate when \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) is added to the given mixture, we compare the solubility product (\( K_{sp} \)) of the sulfides formed with the sulfide ion concentration. The sulfides with \( K_{sp} \) values less than the sulfide ion concentration will precipitate.

1. **Solubility Products of Sulfides:**
   - \( \text{MnS} \): \( K_{sp} = 10^{-14} \)
   - \( \text{NiS} \): \( K_{sp} = 10^{-21} \)
   - \( \text{CuS} \): \( K_{sp} = 10^{-21} \)
   - \( \text{HgS} \): \( K_{sp} = 10^{-{53}} \)

2. **Acidified Solution:**
   - The \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas in an acidified solution dissociates to release sulfide ions (\( \text{S}^{2-} \)):
     \[
     \text{H}_2\text{S} \rightleftharpoons 2\text{H}^+ + \text{S}^{2-}
     \]
   - The \( \text{H}^+ \) ions from the acid suppress the dissociation of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \), but the sulfide ion concentration is still measurable.

3. **Comparison:**
   - \( \text{HgS} \) has the smallest \( K_{sp} \), making it the least soluble sulfide. It will precipitate first.
   - \( \text{NiS} \) and \( \text{CuS} \) have very low \( K_{sp} \) values, so they will also precipitate.
   - \( \text{MnS} \) has a higher \( K_{sp} \), so it is likely to remain in solution.

4. **Conclusion:**
   The sulfides that will precipitate are \( \text{HgS} \), \( \text{NiS} \), and \( \text{CuS} \).

\[
\boxed{\text{HgS}, \text{NiS}, \text{CuS}}
\]
