","The terms ""isototopologues' and ""isotopomers"" are used to describe variations in molecules based on isotopic substitution. Here’s the key difference:  
1. **Isototopologues**: These are molecules that differ only in the isotopic composition of their atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_6 \) (normal \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_6 \)) and \( \text{C}_6\text{D}_6 \) (fully deuterated \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_6 \)) are isototopologues because they have the same molecular structure but differ in the isotopic composition of hydrogen (protium vs. deuterium).  
2. **Isotopomers**: These are molecules that differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms but also in their molecular structure. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_6 \) and \( \text{C}_6\text{D}_6 \) are not isotopomers because they have the same molecular structure. However, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_6 \) and \( \text{C}_6\text{D}_5\text{H} \) (a partially deuterated \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_6 \)) are isotopomers because they differ in the number of deuterium atoms and the resulting structural changes.  

Thus, the key difference is that isototopologues have the same molecular structure, while isotopomers may have different molecular structures due to isotopic substitution.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Isototopologues: Same structure, different isotopic composition.} \\
&\text{Isotopomers: Different structure, different isotopic composition.}
\end{aligned}
}
