(v) The volumetric area ratio up to which the lumped parameter method can be used.  

(i) The temperature of the part after 5 minutes.  
The temperature of the part after 5 minutes can be determined using the lumped parameter method, which assumes that the temperature is uniform throughout the body. The temperature equation is:  
\[
T(t) = T_0 + (T_a - T_0) e^{-t/\tau}
\]  
where:  
- \( T(t) \) is the temperature at time \( t \),  
- \( T_0 \) is the initial temperature,  
- \( T_a \) is the ambient temperature,  
- \( \tau \) is the time constant.  

The time constant \( \tau \) is given by:  
\[
\tau = \frac{m c_p}{h A}
\]  
where:  
- \( m \) is the mass,  
- \( c_p \) is the specific heat,  
- \( h \) is the convective heat transfer coefficient,  
- \( A \) is the surface area.  

Substituting the given values:  
\[
\tau = \frac{1.2 \times 385}{56.8 \times 0.075} = 10.5 \text{ min}
\]  

The temperature after 5 minutes is:  
\[
T(5) = 345 + (20 - 345) e^{-5/10.5} = 345 - 325 e^{-0.476} = 345 - 325 \times 0.602 = 345 - 192.6 = 152.4^\circ \text{C}
\]  

(ii) The time required to reach 50°C.  
To find the time required to reach 50°C, we rearrange the lumped parameter equation:  
\[
t = \tau \ln\left(\frac{T_0 - T_a}{T(t) - T_a}\right)
\]  
Substituting the given values:  
\[
t = 10.5 \ln\left(\frac{345 - 20}{50 - 20}\right) = 10.5 \ln(3.25) = 10.5 \times 1.12 = 11.7 \text{ min}
\]  

(iii) The time constant.  
The time constant \( \tau \) is calculated as:  
\[
\tau = \frac{m c_p}{h A} = \frac{1.2 \times 385}{56.8 \times 0.075} = 10.5 \text{ min}
\]  

(iv) The value of convective heat transfer coefficient up to which the lumped parameter model can be used.  
