Consider a p-channel MOSFET with an oxide thickness of 500 Å and a donor doping concentration \(N_d = 10^{16} \text{ cm}^{-3}\). Calculate the body-to-source voltage (\(V_{BS}\)) needed to shift the threshold voltage (\(V_T\)) from its value at \(V_{BS} = 0\) by \(-1.0\) V.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The threshold voltage of a p-channel MOSFET is given by:
\[
V_T = V_{T0} + \alpha V_{BS}
\]
where:
- \(V_{T0}\) is the threshold voltage at \(V_{BS} = 0\),
- \(\alpha\) is the built-over voltage coefficient,
- \(V_{BS}\) is the built-over voltage.

For a p-channel MOSFET, the built-over voltage coefficient is given by:
\[
\alpha = \frac{2eN_d}{C_{ox}}
\]
where:
- \(e\) is the electron charge,
- \(N_d\) is the donor doping concentration,
- \(C_{ox}\) is the oxide capacitance per unit area.

The oxide capacitance per unit area is given by:
\[
C_{ox} = \frac{4\pi e}{t_{ox}}
\]
where:
- \(t_{ox}\) is the oxide thickness.

Substituting the given values:
- \(t_{ox} = 500 \text{ Å} = 500 \times 10^{-10} \text{ cm}\),
- \(N_d = 10^{16} \text{ cm}^{-3}\),
- \(e = 1.602 \times 10^{-19} \text{ C}\),

we get:
\[
C_{ox} = \frac{4\pi (1.602 \times 10^{-19})}{500 \times 10^{-10}} = 1.226 \times 10^{-16} \text{ C/cm}^2
\]
\[
\alpha = \frac{2(1.602 \times 10^{-19})(10^{16})}{1.226 \times 10^{-16}} = 0.205 \text{ V/V}
\]

The threshold voltage is shifted by \(-1.0\) V, so:
\[
V_{BS} = \frac{V_T - V_{T0}}{\alpha} = \frac{-1.0}{0.205} = -4.89 \text{ V}
\]

Thus, the body-to-source voltage required is \(\boxed{-4.89 \text{ V}}\).
