What is the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To determine the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a photon scattered by an electron, we use the Compton effect formula:

\[
\Delta \lambda = \lambda' - \lambda = \frac{2 \pi \hbar}{m_e c} (1 - \cos \theta)
\]

where:
- \(\Delta \lambda = \lambda' - \lambda\) is the change in wavelength,
- \(\lambda'\) is the scattered wavelength,
- \(\lambda = 2.00 \, \text{Å}\) is the initial wavelength,
- \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(1.054 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\)),
- \(m_e\) is the electron mass (\(9.109 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\)),
- \(c\) is the speed of light (\(2.998 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\)),
- \(\theta\) is the scattering angle.

The maximum change occurs when \(\theta = 180^\circ\) (back-forward scattering), so:

\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{2 \pi \hbar}{m_e c} (1 - \cos 180^\circ) = \frac{4 \pi \hbar}{m_e c}
\]

The percentage change is:

\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}}}{\lambda} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{4 \pi \hbar}{m_e c \lambda} \right) \times 100
\]

Substituting the values:

\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{4 \pi \times 1.054 \times 10^{-34}}{9.109 \times 10^{-31} \times 2.998 \times 10^8 \times 2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \right) \times 100
\]

\[
\text{Percentage change} \approx 0.032\%
\]

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength is \(\boxed{0.032\%}\).
