<start_of_turn>model
","The yield strength and percent elongation of an aluminum alloyed with 4% copper (containing hard intermetallic CuAl$_2$ particles) will differ from those of pure aluminum. The yield strength will be higher, and the percent elongation will be lower. 

The reasons for these differences are:
1. **Hard particles (CuAl$_2$) and strain hardening**: The addition of hard CuAl$_2$ particles to the aluminum matrix can significantly increase the yield strength. These particles act as obstacles to dislocation motion, which is the primary mechanism of plastic deformation in metals. As the aluminum deforms, the dislocations must bypass these particles, requiring more stress to do so. This phenomenon is known as strain hardening or work strengthening.
2. **Particle-matrix bonding**: The strength of the particle-matrix bonding also plays a role in determining the overall strength of the alloy. If the particles are well bonded to the aluminum matrix, they can further hinder dislocation motion and increase the yield strength.
3. **Reduced ductility**: The presence of hard particles can also lead to a decrease in the percent elongation of the alloy. This is because the particles can act as stress concentrators, leading to localized deformation and failure at lower strains. As the alloy deforms, the particles can also cause micro-voids to form, further reducing the ductility.

Thus, the 4% copper addition to aluminum, resulting in the formation of hard CuAl$_2$ particles, will increase the yield strength of the alloy but reduce its percent elongation. 

\boxed{\text{The yield strength increases due to strain hardening and particle-matrix bonding, while the percent elongation decreases due to particle-induced stress concentration and micro-voiding.}}
