","The typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy is \(\boxed{\pm 1.1 \text{ MeV}}\). This is derived from the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle, which states that the product of the uncertainty in energy (\(\Delta E\)) and the uncertainty in time (\(\Delta t\)) must satisfy:
\[
\Delta E \cdot \Delta t \geq \frac{\hbar}{2}
\]
where \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(\hbar = \frac{h}{2\pi} = 1.054 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}\)).

Given the lifetime \(\Delta t = 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}\), we can solve for the uncertainty in energy:
\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{\hbar}{2 \Delta t} = \frac{1.054 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{2 \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 2.635 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J}
\]
Convert this energy to MeV:
\[
\Delta E = 2.635 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}} = 4.05 \text{ MeV}
\]
Thus, the typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy is:
\[
1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 4.05 \text{ MeV} = 1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 1.1 \text{ MeV}
\]
The uncertainty is dominated by the lifetime of the Σ⁺ particle.
