<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The proton is subject to a force \(F = qE\) due to the electric field, where \(q = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) and \(E = 500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
The net force causes the proton to accelerate, and the acceleration \(a\) is given by Newton's second law:  
\[
F = ma \implies a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{qE}{m} = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 3.81 \times 10^{11} \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]  
Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{3.81 \times 10^{11} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  

(b) The proton starts from rest (\(u = 0 \, \text{m/s}\)) and accelerates to a speed \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\).  
Using the kinematic equation \(v^2 = u^2 + 2ad\), where \(d\) is the distance traveled, we solve for \(d\):  
\[
d = \frac{v^2 - u^2}{2a} = \frac{(35,000 \, \text{m/s})^2 - (0 \, \text{m/s})^2}{2(3.81 \times 10^{11} \, \text{m/s}^2)} = 5.08 \times 10^2 \, \text{m}.
\]  
The time \(t\) taken to travel this distance is:  
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0 \, \text{m/s}}{3.81 \times 10^{11} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 9.16 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{s}.
\]  
Thus, the time taken is \(\boxed{9.16 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{s}}\).
