","The net chemical reaction during the discharging of a lead storage battery is:
\[ \text{Pb(s)} + \text{PbO}_2(s) + 2\text{H}^+(aq) + 2\text{e}^- \rightarrow 2\text{Pb(OH)}_2(s) \]

This reaction can be broken down into two half-reactions:
1. **Oxidation at the anode (Pb electrode):**
   \[ \text{Pb(s)} + \text{H}^+(aq) + \text{e}^- \rightarrow \text{Pb(OH)}_2(s) \]
   Here, lead metal is oxidized to lead(II) hydroxide.
2. **Reduction at the cathode (PbO₂ electrode):**
   \[ \text{PbO}_2(s) + 2\text{H}^+(aq) + 2\text{e}^- \rightarrow \text{Pb(OH)}_2(s) \]
   Here, lead(IV) oxide is reduced to lead(II) hydroxide.

The overall reaction is the sum of these two half-reactions, with the electrons canceling out. The lead(II) hydroxide formed during the discharge process can be reconverted back to lead and lead(IV) oxide by passing an electric current through the battery, which is the process of charging the battery. 

Thus, the net chemical reaction during the discharging of a lead storage battery is:
\[ \boxed{\text{Pb(s)} + \text{PbO}_2(s) + 2\text{H}^+(aq) + 2\text{e}^- \rightarrow 2\text{Pb(OH)}_2(s)} \]
