1. ADP + Pi = ATP (ΔG° = -30.5 kJ/mol)
2. ATP + H2O = ADP + Pi (ΔG° = ?)

Since the first step is the reverse of the second step, the standard free energy change for the hydrolysis of ATP (ΔG° for step 2) will be the negative of the given value for the first step:

ΔG° = -(-30.5 kJ/mol) = 30.5 kJ/mol

Now we can plug this value into the Gibbs-Helmholtz equation:

ΔG = 30.5 kJ/mol + (8.314 J/mol·K)(310 K) ln(0.001)

ΔG = 30.5 kJ/mol + (-1.99 kJ/mol)

ΔG = 28.51 kJ/mol

The standard free energy change for the hydrolysis of ATP at 37°C with the given concentrations is 28.51 kJ/mol.
